669921875, -0.007228851318359375, 0.004917144775390625, 0.016510009765625, -0.0164794921875, -0.0164337158203125, 0.036712646484375, 0.0148468017578125, 0.0489501953125, -0.002658843994140625, 0.0079193115234375, -0.0225372314453125, 0.0380859375, 0.013336181640625, 0.004711151123046875, 0.014923095703125, -0.0272674560546875, -0.01171112060546875, 0.005153656005859375, -0.0011758804321289062, -0.0150146484375, 0.0245819091796875, 3.8623809814453125e-05, 0.040496826171875, -0.020538330078125, 0.0003490447998046875, 0.00014865398406982422, 0.0037708282470703125, 0.0230865478515625, -0.020538330078125, 0.006732940673828125, -0.01113128662109375, -0.0240631103515625, 0.033233642578125, -0.0232086181640625, -0.0217742919921875, 0.0167694091796875, -0.00543975830078125, 0.0201568603515625, 0.0127105712890625, 0.036468505859375, 0.0369873046875, -0.0140838623046875, -0.0100250244140625, 0.0009593963623046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose  \nTo develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods  \nStudy Design and Data: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single institution in 2023 and 2024.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline: The system consisted of two primary agent types: (1) a rule-based agent using regular expressions (regex) to identify and segment sentences into standardized anatomical sections, and (2) a local large language model (LLM) agent to resolve ambiguous or complex sentence assignments and to perform semantic checks. The agents operated sequentially, with the regex agent first parsing the report and the LLM agent reviewing and refining section assignments and performing quality assurance (QA) checks. The rationale for combining regex and LLM approaches was to leverage the precision of rule-based parsing for well-structured content and the flexibility of LLMs for handling variability in reporting language.\n\nQuality Assurance Tasks: The system flagged reports for (a) mismatches between Findings and Impression sections or within sections, (b) gender-anatomy conflicts (e.g., references to male anatomy in female patients), and (c) undocumented communication of critical findings.\n\nEvaluation: Two board-certified radiologists independently reviewed a randomly selected subset of 45 reports (stratified to include both flagged and unflagged cases). They assessed (1) the correctness of restructuring (whether anatomical sectioning was accurate and original content preserved), (2) QA performance (whether flagged issues were clinically relevant and correctly identified), and (3) whether any clinically important information was omitted or fabricated. Disagreements were resolved by consensus discussion. Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic.\n\nResults  \nReport Structuring: The system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format, retaining original content.\n\nFlagged Errors: The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%): 80 (12.5%) for section mismatches (e.g., findings present in one section but not reflected in the Impression), 7 (1.1%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 3 (0.5%) for undocumented critical findings. Most section mismatches were minor (e.g., omission of benign findings from the Impression), but 12 involved potentially clinically significant discrepancies.\n\nRadiologist Evaluation: Both reviewers agreed that 31/45 reports (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2/45 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and disagreed on 12/45 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%). Cohen\u2019s kappa for restructuring correctness was 0.62 (substantial agreement). Both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted or fabricated. QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" or \"good\" in 38/45 reports (84%, 95% CI: 70\u201393%), and \"fair\" in 7/45 (16%, 95% CI: 7\u201330%).\n\nLimitations  \nThis study is limited by its retrospective design, single-institution data, and potential overfitting to local reporting styles. The sample size for radiologist evaluation was modest, and the system\u2019s generalizability to other institutions or modalities remains untested.\n\nConclusion  \nA locally deployed multi-agent AI system combining rule-based and LLM agents enabled radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. The system demonstrated promising performance in a limited radiologist evaluation, but further validation on larger, multi-institutional datasets is needed to confirm its generalizability and clinical impact. Such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice."}}